hown some covid  carriers are infectious before they show  symptoms making the spread even harder  to contain  and that's why we ask people when  they've potentially had contact  to self-isolate regardless of whether  they're currently testing positive or  not because we're still missing  that critical piece of information  before you develop symptoms  while the virus spreads quickly the  lancet research also found that no  live virus remained in symptomatic  patients after nine days  so the infectiousness to others does  drop off dramatically  one other new study says covet carriers  develop immunity that is now shown to  last at least  six months this is potentially  significant news it extends the period  for which we know there is likely  protection  evidence of a strong immune response  gives researchers optimism  that vaccines now in development will  also have lasting immunity  eric sorensen global news toronto  the verdict many victims families didn't  want coming up a jury finds the  fredericton shooter  not criminally responsible  two years ago fredericton was rocked by  a mass shooting  matthew raymond shot and killed four  people including two police officers  today a jury found him not criminally  responsible because of a mental disorder  for more on the verdict and how the  community is reacting  let's go to global's silas brown silas  it took the 11-member jury 25 hours over  the course of four days to reach a  verdict  after the verdict was read raymond could  be seen wiping tears from his eyes  and some members of the victim's  families ran from the room  and could be heard sobbing in the  hallway jackie mclean is the widow  of rob costello one of the police  officers killed  i was hopeful that he would be found  responsible for it  only because  i don't think he's acknowledged  responsibility or his  his responsibility for this here's how  the case has unfolded  raymond faced four counts of  first-degree murder dating back to the  morning of august 10  2018 when he opened fire from inside his  fredericton apartment  the shooting began just after seven in  the morning and lasted less than half an  hour  donald robichaud and bobby lee wright  were shot as they packed up their car  for a camping trip  fredericton police constables sarah  burns and rob costello were the first  officers on scene  responding to the active shooter call  and were shot before they could draw  their weapons  that raymond shot and killed the four  victims was never in dispute  his lawyers admitted as much but they  argued that raymond should be found not  criminally responsible for his actions  due to a mental disorder and three  separate forensic psychiatrists have  diagnosed raymond with either  schizophrenia  or delusional disorder during the trial  but the central issue the jury was  tasked with deciding  is if that mental disorder impaired  raymond's ability to understand the  nature  and quality of his actions or that they  were wrong raymond testified that he  believed the end times had begun  and that he was under attack by demons  having barricaded himself inside his  apartment  with loaded weapons for days the defense  has argued that this is supported by the  mountain of electronic data  the crown has acknowledged that raymond  was experiencing delusions the day of  the shooting  but says that they were less intense  than he claims and that he targeted the  four victims knowingly  raymond will now be sent to new  brunswick's psychiatric hospital  in campbellton where he will await a  disposition hearing in december  that will decide if he is treated at the  federal or provincial facility  robin silas brown in fredericton new  brunswick tonight  still ahead donald trump's new strategy  to ignore american democracy  the cases of kofi 19 in the united  states are soaring to new levels  more than 80 000 people have been  hospitalized that's the most since the  pandemic  began the u.s has also set a new daily  covid19 record  more than 187 000 people tested positive  yesterday the u.s president is showing  unprecedented disregard for democracy  today donald trump met with state  lawmakers from michigan in a bid to  convince them to ignore the will of  voters  let's bring in our washington bureau  chief jackson proskow jackson what is  trump's strategy here  well robin simply put trump wants  republican-controlled state legislatures  in key swing states to ignore the fact  that voters picked joe biden and instead  appoint a new slate of electors to the  electoral college  remember it's those electors who  ultimately go on to pick the president  those two michigan lawmakers you  mentioned flew to washington today to  meet with president trump  it's not clear what they discussed but  it is clear that this is part of a  maximum pressure campaign  to get several states to ignore the  wishes of voters  shortly before that meeting we should  point out that trump again insisted he  won the election  robin it's very clear he did not but he  continues to argue that there was some  kind of voter fraud  is there proof  no there is no proof at this point and  not only has he not  offered proof of the 30 lawsuits filed  by the trump campaign  only one has been successful most of  those complaints have fallen apart or  simply been withdrawn due to a sheer  lack of evidence  tonight the state of georgia though is  moving ahead with certifying its results  in favor of joe biden  they found no fraud or irregularities  that would stop that from happening  meanwhile biden's team seems to be  losing patience with the stalled  transition  the president-elect today met with  democratic congressional leaders like  nancy pelosi  they together have not ruled out going  to the courts to compel  trump to cooperate with the transition  process a courtesy we should point out  that was extended to trump by outgoing  president barack obama four years ago  jackson prescott washington thanks  jackson  up next could a train project in ontario  derail the health of people  living nearby  one effective way to reduce greenhouse  gas emissions is transporting goods  across the country by train  instead of truck cn has been pushing to  do that for 20 years  it wants to build a terminal where  shipping containers can be transferred  onto trains  but some residents in milton ontario the  proposed site  aren't on board jeff semple explains  priscilla burton's five-year-old son  suffers from asthma  and their home town of milton an hour's  drive west of toronto  offers a breath of fresh air we had more  access to like  countryside and farms and conservation  areas  but burton's family like many others  here is worried  about a new neighbor poised to move in  down the street  cn the canadian national railway  purchased this stretch of land  with plans to build an intermodal  terminal  the facilities provide an environmental  benefit by taking long-haul trucks  off the road and transferring goods onto  trains  it's a great idea i fully support it  there's just one problem with milton  it's the wrong place this transportation  analyst  says most terminals like this one in  nearby brampton are  built in industrial areas but in milton  it's a much different story  cn's proposed site here in milton sits  just across the street from a sprawling  residential area within a kilometer of  thousands of residents  a hospital and a dozen schools  the site would see 800 transport trucks  rolling in and out of the area every day  an independent government review panel  found most of the adverse environmental  effects from the terminal are  likely to occur anyway because the lands  have been designated for future  development  but the panel concluded the terminal is  likely to cause significant adverse  environmental effects on air quality  and on human health as it relates to air  quality i'm not just talking about one  or two and i'm talking about the masses  of the people just don't want it but in  this case the community  doesn't get to decide the rules  governing rail companies  are as old as canada written back in  1867 the constitution ensured railways  were the domain of the federal  government  as a result cn's fate falls to the feds  this could be saskatoon this could be  vancouver  it could be halifax it can be anywhere  cnn declined our request for an  interview but noted the review panel  also found  cn's criteria for site selection were  reasonable and the proposed site could  support an intermodal terminal  they're kind of putting their company's  interest ahead of our health the  government's decision is expected within  a month  jeff semple global news milton ontario  and tomorrow an environmental health  expert who studies cancer risks weighs  in with his take on the risk posed by  pollution from the proposed terminal  that's on our program the new reality  saturday at 7 pm with donna friesen  right here on global and that is global  national for this friday i'm robin gill  tonight your canada  is cashabog lake in ontario thank you  for watching and i'll see you here this  weekend have a great night
 

